    <span class="mono-label">GUIDE</span>
    <h1>What should I do this summer if I didn't get an internship?</h1>

    <p class="answer-capsule">Don't spend it waiting. The best use of a summer without an internship is to build proof of work: pick the role you want, complete a few real tasks from it, and get them evaluated against a professional standard. A summer of scored work in your target field is a stronger signal than a blank line on a résumé, and you do not need an employer's permission to build it.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <ul>
        <li>An internship matters mostly because of the proof it produces, and you can produce comparable proof on your own.</li>
        <li>The stakes are real: completing an internship <a href="https://www.strada.org/reports/talent-disrupted" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cuts underemployment risk by 48.5%</a>, and your first job after graduation shapes the next ten years (Strada and Burning Glass).</li>
        <li>The fix for a missing internship is the same thing that makes internships valuable: real, evaluated work in your target role.</li>
        <li>Aim for a few strong, relevant, scored pieces, not a pile of scattered projects.</li>
        <li>Make the work count by getting it in front of recruiters, not leaving it in a folder.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Why does a blank summer hurt your chances?</h2>
      <p>Because the market reads gaps as missing evidence, and the timing compounds. The <em>Talent Disrupted</em> research found that students who start their careers underemployed are <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/underemployment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3.5 times more likely to still be underemployed a decade later</a>, which means the early signals you build matter far beyond the next application. An internship is the traditional way to build those signals, and the data shows it lowers underemployment risk by nearly half. So a summer with no internship is not a catastrophe, but it is a missed chance to produce the one thing recruiters most want from a young candidate, which is evidence. The good news is that the missing piece is reproducible.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is the best use of a summer without an internship?</h2>
      <p>Treat the summer as a production sprint, not a holding pattern. The reason an internship helps is not the title, it is that you did real work, someone evaluated it, and now there is something concrete to point to. You can manufacture that same outcome directly. Spend the summer doing the actual work of the role you want, on real, scoped problems, and get each piece evaluated so its quality is established rather than assumed. By September you would have a small body of scored work in your target field, which is exactly the proof an internship leaves behind, built on your own terms.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you choose what to work on?</h2>
      <p>Narrow before you start. Pick one role, the one you would take an entry-level job in today, and do the work of that role rather than sampling five fields. Choose tasks that produce a real artifact: a positioning brief, a product spec, an analysis that ends in a recommendation. Aim for three to five strong pieces, not a dozen weak ones, because a recruiter is trying to answer can this person do this specific job, and a focused body of relevant work answers that far better than a scattered one. While building zero, our Middle East university pilot in Spring 2026 made this concrete: students learned and improved fastest through short loops of doing real work and getting specific feedback on it, not through broad study. Pick the role, do the work, get it scored, repeat.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you make the summer count for hiring?</h2>
      <p>Get the work seen. A summer of scored work only changes your odds if recruiters can find and evaluate it, so build it somewhere that puts evaluated talent in front of the people who hire. That is what zero is for: you take on company-shaped tasks, your work is scored against a professional bar, and the scored result becomes a receipt recruiters can evaluate, free for students because recruiters pay for the access. Used that way, a summer without an internship can produce more hiring signal than the internship you did not get.</p>
    </section>

